# Shuffling Bases: Quadratic Exchange in Matroids

This interactive companion lets you *play* with the mathematics behind White's Quadratic Exchange Conjecture (Part 3). We begin with the single move that generates everything, then climb to the structural theorems and the complete solution in rank one.

## 1. The fingerprint that never changes

Every configuration of bases carries a conserved quantity — its **multiset union**, or fingerprint: the pool of all elements across all bases, counted with multiplicity. Every legal move preserves it exactly, so two configurations can be connected only if their fingerprints match. The algorithm below computes it in linear time.

{{algorithm:0}}

## 2. One move at a time

The atomic operation is the **quadratic exchange move**: pull two bases out, repack their pooled elements into two new bases of the right size. In a uniform matroid *every* such repacking is legal. Try it yourself — pick two bases and watch all legal successors appear, with the fingerprint held fixed.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

Under the hood, this is the move generator:

{{algorithm:1}}

And here it is as a standalone demo you can run:

{{demo:1}}

## 3. Do moves compose? The congruence principle

A move legal on two bases stays legal amid untouched bases — reachability is a **congruence**. This is what lets us solve big reconfigurations by gluing small ones. The reachability solver below performs a breadth-first search over the move graph and prints an explicit sequence connecting your two configurations.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

The search itself:

{{algorithm:2}}

We can also map the entire move graph for a fixed fingerprint and confirm it is connected:

